<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pharisees</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pharisees were a Jewish religious and political party of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> period, first attested under the Hasmoneans in the 2nd century BCE. They upheld an oral tradition of interpretation alongside the written Torah and believed in the resurrection of the dead. After the destruction of the Temple in 70 CE their teachings shaped rabbinic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pharisees.jpg" alt="Pharisees" width="330" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Duccio di Buoninsegna · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duccio_di_Buoninsegna_-_Christ_Accused_by_the_Pharisees_(detail)_-_WGA06802.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Pharisee comes from the Greek Pharisaios, from an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> word meaning set apart. It may refer to their separation from Gentiles and sources of ritual impurity, or from irreligious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. The Pharisees emerged largely from the scribes and sages and were active from the middle of the 2nd century BCE until the destruction of the Temple in 70 CE. Josephus first mentions them in connection with Jonathan Apphus, successor of Judas Maccabaeus.</p><p>The Pharisees held that all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> had to observe the purity laws, which applied to the Temple service, outside the Temple. They also stood out for their adherence to the laws and traditions of the Jewish people in the face of assimilation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> considered them the most expert and accurate expositors of Jewish law, and estimated that there were about 6,000 of them before the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the priestly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty">Hasmonean dynasty</a> the Pharisees did not support the wars of expansion or the forced conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaeans">Idumeans</a>. The rift widened when a Pharisee named Eleazar insulted the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus">John Hyrcanus</a> at his table. Hyrcanus's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-jannaeus">Alexander Jannaeus</a> sided openly with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadducees">Sadducees</a>, and the resulting riot and brief civil war ended in a bloody repression of the Pharisees. His widow, Salome Alexandra, was favourably inclined towards them, and under her reign their influence grew greatly, especially in the Sanhedrin, which they came to dominate.</p><p>After the Jewish–Roman wars the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zealots">Zealots</a> were crushed, the last dying at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a> in 73 CE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadducees">Sadducees</a>, whose teachings were tied to the Temple, disappeared with its destruction. Of the major sects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> period, only the Pharisees remained.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> says the Pharisees held that people have free will but that God also foresees human destiny. The sources differ on their view of the afterlife: according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> they believed in the resurrection of the dead, while Josephus says they held the soul to be immortal. They applied Jewish law to everyday life, in a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> that did not depend on the Temple and in which leaders were determined by scholarly achievement rather than birth. The standard view is that they accepted an Oral Torah alongside the written Scripture, though Anthony J. Saldarini argued that there is no direct evidence for this.</p><p>Their view of law as a way for ordinary people to engage with the sacred in daily life let them answer the questions raised by the loss of the Temple, and their responses formed Rabbinic Judaism. The Pharisees appear in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> in conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, but Nicodemus and Gamaliel were Pharisees, and Paul the Apostle declared himself one.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pharisees” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the following centuries, under Persian, Greek and then Roman rule, the scriptures were gathered and edited and the religion of the Second Temple period developed, with its priests, its scribes and its rival schools: the <b>Pharisees</b>, the Sadducees, the Essenes and others.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pharisees</b>, Sadducees and Essenes emerged in this era.</blockquote><a href="/state/hasmonean-kingdom">Hasmonean Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
